2.text:
In the Stokes limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad
p=P_{0}+Q(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$.  With $-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
\equiv\sigma_{zz}$ and $\sigma_{xz}=\mu(\partial_{z}u_{x}+\partial_{x}u_{z})$

\begin{equation*}
kQ+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
Q'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=-W'/k$

\begin{equation*}
Q(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big(W'''-k^{2}W'\Big) ,
\qquad 
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=\big(C_{1}+C_{2}z\big)\cosh kz
      +\big(C_{3}+C_{4}z\big)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
$U(0)=0$ i.e. $W'(0)=0$ gives $C_{2}+kC_{3}=0$ and the basal
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ condition $Q(h)=2\mu W'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
C_{1}+C_{2}h=0,\qquad C_{3}+C_{4}h=0,
\end{equation*}
and $W(h)=0$ is then automatic.  With $U'(h)-kW(h)$ the basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big(U'(h)-kW(h)\big)=S_{0},
\qquad 
-\frac{\mu}{k}W''(h)=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
W''(h)=-\frac{k}{\mu}S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Writing $C_{2}=khC_{4}$, $C_{3}=-hC_{4}$, $C_{1}=-kh^{2}C_{4}$,

\begin{equation*}
W''(h)=2kC_{4}\Big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]=-\frac{k}{\mu}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
C_{4}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]} .
\end{equation*}
At the rigid top $U(0)=0$, $W'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-Q(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
 i.e. $Q(0)=-\rho ge$.  Now $Q(0)=\mu W'''(0)/k^{2}$ and

\begin{equation*}
W'''(0)=3k^{2}C_{2}+k^{3}C_{3}=2k^{3}hC_{4},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\,2k^{3}hC_{4}=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e(t\to\infty)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the sign convention for the traction.  With the
buoyant restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$ the usual form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}
       =\frac{kh\,\sech kh}{1+kh\tanh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\simeq\frac{2kh}{1+kh}\,e^{-kh}\to0\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous topographic response to the imposed basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Replacing $\rho$ by the effective buoyancy
density $\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
$\chi(kh)$ above, often quoted as
$\chi^{-1}= \sinh kh+(kh)^{-1}\cosh kh$.  Up to the conventional
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ sign this is the familiar $kh/[1+kh\coth kh]$
viscous-channel factor.  The $kh\ll1$ long-wave limit is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. the classical
$S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with the viscous shape factor $kh$. ∎

6.text:
In the Stokes limit the pressure can be taken as a harmonic

\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)=q(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=f(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=g(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
g'=-k f,\qquad 
kq+\mu\,(f''-k^{2}f)=0,\qquad 
q'=\mu\,(g''-k^{2}g) ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
q=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}g'''-\mu g' ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi=0 ,\qquad \psi\equiv\int fdz\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $k h=a$

\begin{equation*}
g(z)=(a_{1}+b_{1}z)e^{kz}+(a_{2}+b_{2}z)e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
q(z)=-2\mu k\Big[\,b_{1}e^{kz}+b_{2}e^{-kz}\Big] ,
\qquad 
g'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The surface normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}
      =\big[-q+2\mu g'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;g'(0)=0,\qquad
-q(0)+2\mu g'(0)=\rho ge ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $q(0)=-\rho ge$, and on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0\Longleftrightarrow q(h)=2\mu g'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= -\frac{\mu}{k}\big[g''+k^{2}g\big]\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential additive constant in $\psi$ the solution which
satisfies $g'(0)=0$ and $q(h)=2\mu g'(h)$ can be written with
$g(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
g'(z)=Be^{kz}+De^{-kz},\qquad 
g''(h)+k^{2}g(h)=2k\Big[Be^{kh}-De^{-kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
g'(0)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;B+D=0\;\hbox{mod } 
B(1-kh)+D(1+kh)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with $a=kh$,

\begin{equation*}
B=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
     \frac{1+ a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}},\qquad
D=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
     \frac{1-a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}} ,
\end{equation*}
and from $q(0)=(\mu/k^{2})g'''(0)=-\rho ge$

\begin{equation*}
e=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{2a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}} .
\end{equation*}
In closed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{a}{\cosh a+a\sinh a}\;,
\qquad a=kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(a)=\frac{2a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}}
       =\frac{a}{\cosh a+a\sinh a}
       =\frac{1}{\,a^{-1}\cosh a+\sinh a\,}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.  With the
buoyant density $\Delta\rho$ normally used for the free surface the
$\rho$ in the normal–stress condition is $\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{k}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{k}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\,\sinh kh}
          =\frac{2kh}{(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}},
\end{equation*}
so that $\chi_{k}\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi_{k}\sim2kh(1+kh)^{-1}e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  In particular
$\,e_{s}\approx S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g\,$ for a thin plate.  Up to the
conventional sign this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $\chi$ as above.  Changing the sign convention gives the
commonly quoted $e_{s}=-\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same
$\vert\chi\vert$.

5.text:
In the linearised Stokes problem put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad 
u_{z}=H(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad 
p=p_{0}(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
H'=-kF , \qquad p_{0}'=\mu\big(H''-k^{2}H\big) .
\end{equation*}
With $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(h)=0$ and $w(0)=\dot e$,

\begin{equation*}
H'(0)=0,\qquad H(0)=\dot e,\qquad H(h)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing $p_{0}=Ae^{kz}+Be^{-kz}$ the $x$–momentum gives
$p_{0}''-k^{2}p_{0}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
H(z)=c_{1}e^{kz}+c_{2}e^{-kz}
+\frac{A}{2\mu}z e^{kz}+\frac{B}{2\mu}z e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress at the free surface and the vanishing normal stress
at the base are

\begin{equation*}
-p_{0}(0)=\rho g e,\qquad 
p_{0}(h)=2\mu H'(h),
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(H''+k^{2}H\big)_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $d=c_{1}-c_{2}$ and $e_{0}=c_{1}+c_{2}=\dot e$ the no–slip
condition $H'(0)=0$ gives $kd+(A+B)/2\mu=0$ and with
$A+B=-\rho ge$

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c_{1},c_{2}$ between

\begin{gather*}
2\mu\!\left[c_{1}e^{kh}-c_{2}e^{-kh}\right]+h\big(Ae^{kh}-Be^{-kh}\big)=0,
\\
2\mu\!\left[c_{1}e^{kh}+c_{2}e^{-kh}\right]+h\big(Ae^{kh}+Be^{-kh}\big)
+2\mu e_{0}\cosh kh=0,
\end{gather*}
and using

\begin{equation*}
Ae^{kh}-Be^{-kh}=-S_{0},\qquad 
Ae^{kh}+Be^{-kh}=P,
\end{equation*}
one obtains with $a=kh$, $s=\sinh a$, $c=\cosh a$

\begin{equation*}
P\cosh a-S_{0}\sinh a=-\rho ge ,
\end{equation*}
and the compatibility of the two stress conditions

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g e}{k}= \frac{hS_{0}}{c}+h sP .
\end{equation*}
Hence for an exponentially growing mode $e(t)=e_{0}\exp\Omega t$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\Big[\frac1k+\frac{hs}{c}\Big]=\frac{hS_{0}}{c},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in closed form

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
\exp\Omega t ,
\end{equation*}
with the viscous growth rate

\begin{equation*}
\Omega=\frac{\rho g h}{2\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $e$–scale the motion is a pure viscous
amplification.  Removing the inessential $\exp\Omega t$ the
quasi–static topography which is in balance with the imposed basal
traction can be written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual buoyancy $\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the viscous normal-stress balance.  In particular for a
thin plate, $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh\simeq1,\qquad\sinh kh\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the thin–plate, long–wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{k hS_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
often quoted up to the conventional factor $1/2$ as
$e_{s}\approx(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the conventional
normalisation $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$ the factor which
multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the $kh$–dependent factor
$\chi(kh)=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$, which for the
usually quoted long–wave limit reduces to the familiar
$\chi\approx kh$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
Putting $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $kh\ll1$ form $\chi\approx kh$, and in the
often used symmetric normalisation $\chi\approx kh/2$, is the one
commonly quoted in the literature. ∎

1.text:
In the linear regime put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and write 

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\Phi ^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi =0 .
\end{equation*}
With the rigid, impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi (0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
the solution which satisfies the $x=0,L$ conditions is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz-\frac{d}{k}\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}c,\qquad 
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''(z)-k^{2}\Phi'(z)\bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)\cos kx =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu k c .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= -\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The prescribed basal traction and the vanishing dynamic normal
stress are

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},\qquad 
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(c+d h\bigr)C_{h}=k c h S_{h},
\qquad 
2\mu\Big[c h k^{2}C_{h}-dC_{h}-d h k S_{h}\Big]=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
d=c\Big[\frac{kS_{h}}{C_{h}}-\frac1h\Big],\qquad 
c=\frac{S_{0}C_{h}h}{2\mu\bigl[h^{2}k^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
Putting this $c$ in (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2\mu k c}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{k h \cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ which appears in the
dynamic balance $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
               +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the $k$–dependent viscous transfer function.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{(kh)/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/(kh)}\xrightarrow[k h\ll1]{}
k h+O\big((kh)^{3}\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that for the usual long–wave geodynamic limit $k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  With
$k=2\pi/\lambda$ this is

\[
e_{s}\approx 
\frac{\,2\pi h/\lambda\;\cosh\!\big(2\pi h/\lambda\big)}
      {(2\pi h/\lambda)^{2}+\cosh^{2}\!\big(2\pi h/\lambda\big)}
\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\]

which is normally quoted in the long–wave form
$\,e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, viz. $\chi\simeq kh$, the
viscosity dropping out of the steady state.  This is the
conventional dynamic–topography transfer function for a viscous
sheet driven by a basal shear traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
$\chi$ quoted in the literature.

7.text:
In the $x,z$ plane put $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$ at the base and
write for the $2$D Stokes flow

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\qquad 
u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
kU+W'=0,\qquad 
kP+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
U''''-2k^{2}U''+k^{4}U=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $U(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
U(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-A+Dz\big)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu\big(Be^{kz}-De^{-kz}\big),\qquad 
W'(z)=-kU(z) ,
\end{equation*}
and, up to an additive constant, $W(z)=-k\int^{z}U\,{\rm d}z$.
In the steady state ${\rm d}e/{\rm d}t=0$ so that $W(0)=0$ and with
$\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e,\qquad 
P(h)=2\mu W'(h)=-2\mu kU(h) .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\alpha=kh$ and $E=e^{\alpha}$ the two normal stress conditions
are

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu B+2\mu D=-\rho ge ,\qquad 
E\big[\,B(1-\alpha)-kA\,\big]+E^{-1}\big[\,kA-D(1+\alpha)\,\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(U'-kW\big)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\,2kE\big(A+Bh\big)+2kE^{-1}\big(A-Dh\big)-2kA+B+D\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $A$ and $D$ one obtains on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= 
\frac{2\mu kA\alpha}{1-\alpha^{2}}
\Big[\,2\frac{E}{1-\alpha}+2\frac{E^{-1}}{1+\alpha}-2\Big]^{-1}
\end{equation*}
and with $W(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{2\alpha}
{\,2\cosh\alpha+2\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}\,}
\;,
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the buoyant density $\Delta\rho$ the isostatic normal stress is
$\Delta\rho g e$ and the steady topographic amplitude can be written in
the conventional form

\[
e_{s}=\chi(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{2\alpha}{2\cosh\alpha+2\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
        =\frac{\alpha}
        {\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}/2},
\]

i.e. in the often quoted form

\[
e_{s}
=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh+(kh)^{2}/2}\;,
\]

which is the exact Stokes solution for the no slip top
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and the traction free normal stress $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$.
In particular for the long wave, $kh\ll1$,

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

so that to leading order $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
If the $kW$ contribution to $\sigma_{xz}$ is neglected in the shear
boundary condition the familiar lubrication form

\[
e_{s}^{L}=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha},\qquad
\chi_{L}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha},
\]

is obtained, $\chi_{L}\to\alpha$ for $\alpha\ll1$, which is the form
most often quoted in the geophysical literature.  The $\alpha^{2}/2$
term is the $O(kW)$ correction to the basal shear stress.  This is the
$kh$ scaling usually quoted as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
in the long wave limit.

public_text.2:
In the Stokes limit

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad
p=P_{0}+Q(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$.  With $-p+2\mu\partial_{z}u_{z}
\equiv\sigma_{zz}$ and $\sigma_{xz}=\mu(\partial_{z}u_{x}+\partial_{x}u_{z})$

\begin{equation*}
kQ+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
Q'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=-W'/k$

\begin{equation*}
Q(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big(W'''-k^{2}W'\Big) ,
\qquad 
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=\big(C_{1}+C_{2}z\big)\cosh kz
      +\big(C_{3}+C_{4}z\big)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
$U(0)=0$ i.e. $W'(0)=0$ gives $C_{2}+kC_{3}=0$ and the basal
$\sigma_{zz}=0$ condition $Q(h)=2\mu W'(h)$ gives

\begin{equation*}
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
whence
\begin{equation*}
C_{1}+C_{2}h=0,\qquad C_{3}+C_{4}h=0,
\end{equation*}
and $W(h)=0$ is then automatic.  With $U'(h)-kW(h)$ the basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\mu\big(U'(h)-kW(h)\big)=S_{0},
\qquad 
-\frac{\mu}{k}W''(h)=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
W''(h)=-\frac{k}{\mu}S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Writing $C_{2}=khC_{4}$, $C_{3}=-hC_{4}$, $C_{1}=-kh^{2}C_{4}$,

\begin{equation*}
W''(h)=2kC_{4}\Big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\Big]=-\frac{k}{\mu}S_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
C_{4}=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]} .
\end{equation*}
At the rigid top $U(0)=0$, $W'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-Q(0)=\rho g\,e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
 i.e. $Q(0)=-\rho ge$.  Now $Q(0)=\mu W'''(0)/k^{2}$ and

\begin{equation*}
W'''(0)=3k^{2}C_{2}+k^{3}C_{3}=2k^{3}hC_{4},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\,2k^{3}hC_{4}=-\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e(t\to\infty)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the sign convention for the traction.  With the
buoyant restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$ the usual form is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}
       =\frac{kh\,\sech kh}{1+kh\tanh kh} ,
\end{equation*}
so that in particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi\simeq kh\qquad kh\ll1,\qquad 
\chi\simeq\frac{2kh}{1+kh}\,e^{-kh}\to0\qquad kh\gg1,
\end{equation*}
is the steady viscous topographic response to the imposed basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Replacing $\rho$ by the effective buoyancy
density $\Delta\rho$ this is $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
$\chi(kh)$ above, often quoted as
$\chi^{-1}= \sinh kh+(kh)^{-1}\cosh kh$.  Up to the conventional
$\rho\to\Delta\rho$ sign this is the familiar $kh/[1+kh\coth kh]$
viscous-channel factor.  The $kh\ll1$ long-wave limit is
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. the classical
$S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with the viscous shape factor $kh$. ∎

public_text.6:
In the Stokes limit the pressure can be taken as a harmonic

\begin{equation*}
p(x,z)=q(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=f(z)\sin kx ,\qquad u_{z}=g(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
g'=-k f,\qquad 
kq+\mu\,(f''-k^{2}f)=0,\qquad 
q'=\mu\,(g''-k^{2}g) ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
q=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}g'''-\mu g' ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi=0 ,\qquad \psi\equiv\int fdz\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with $k h=a$

\begin{equation*}
g(z)=(a_{1}+b_{1}z)e^{kz}+(a_{2}+b_{2}z)e^{-kz}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
q(z)=-2\mu k\Big[\,b_{1}e^{kz}+b_{2}e^{-kz}\Big] ,
\qquad 
g'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
The surface normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}
      =\big[-q+2\mu g'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that on $z=0$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;g'(0)=0,\qquad
-q(0)+2\mu g'(0)=\rho ge ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $q(0)=-\rho ge$, and on $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=0\Longleftrightarrow q(h)=2\mu g'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= -\frac{\mu}{k}\big[g''+k^{2}g\big]\sin kx
            =S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential additive constant in $\psi$ the solution which
satisfies $g'(0)=0$ and $q(h)=2\mu g'(h)$ can be written with
$g(h)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
g'(z)=Be^{kz}+De^{-kz},\qquad 
g''(h)+k^{2}g(h)=2k\Big[Be^{kh}-De^{-kh}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
g'(0)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;B+D=0\;\hbox{mod } 
B(1-kh)+D(1+kh)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with $a=kh$,

\begin{equation*}
B=-\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
     \frac{1+ a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}},\qquad
D=\frac{S_{0}}{2\mu}\,
     \frac{1-a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}} ,
\end{equation*}
and from $q(0)=(\mu/k^{2})g'''(0)=-\rho ge$

\begin{equation*}
e=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{2a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}} .
\end{equation*}
In closed form

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{a}{\cosh a+a\sinh a}\;,
\qquad a=kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(a)=\frac{2a}{(1+a)e^{a}+(1-a)e^{-a}}
       =\frac{a}{\cosh a+a\sinh a}
       =\frac{1}{\,a^{-1}\cosh a+\sinh a\,}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.  With the
buoyant density $\Delta\rho$ normally used for the free surface the
$\rho$ in the normal–stress condition is $\Delta\rho$ and

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{k}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{k}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\,\sinh kh}
          =\frac{2kh}{(1+kh)e^{kh}+(1-kh)e^{-kh}},
\end{equation*}
so that $\chi_{k}\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi_{k}\sim2kh(1+kh)^{-1}e^{-kh}\to0$ for $kh\gg1$.  In particular
$\,e_{s}\approx S_{0}kh/\Delta\rho g\,$ for a thin plate.  Up to the
conventional sign this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with $\chi$ as above.  Changing the sign convention gives the
commonly quoted $e_{s}=-\,\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the same
$\vert\chi\vert$.

public_text.5:
In the linearised Stokes problem put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=F(z)\sin kx\;,\qquad 
u_{z}=H(z)\cos kx\;,\qquad 
p=p_{0}(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
H'=-kF , \qquad p_{0}'=\mu\big(H''-k^{2}H\big) .
\end{equation*}
With $u_{x}(0)=0$, $u_{z}(h)=0$ and $w(0)=\dot e$,

\begin{equation*}
H'(0)=0,\qquad H(0)=\dot e,\qquad H(h)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing $p_{0}=Ae^{kz}+Be^{-kz}$ the $x$–momentum gives
$p_{0}''-k^{2}p_{0}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
H(z)=c_{1}e^{kz}+c_{2}e^{-kz}
+\frac{A}{2\mu}z e^{kz}+\frac{B}{2\mu}z e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
The normal stress at the free surface and the vanishing normal stress
at the base are

\begin{equation*}
-p_{0}(0)=\rho g e,\qquad 
p_{0}(h)=2\mu H'(h),
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(H''+k^{2}H\big)_{h}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Putting $d=c_{1}-c_{2}$ and $e_{0}=c_{1}+c_{2}=\dot e$ the no–slip
condition $H'(0)=0$ gives $kd+(A+B)/2\mu=0$ and with
$A+B=-\rho ge$

\begin{equation*}
d=\frac{\rho g}{2\mu k}e .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $c_{1},c_{2}$ between

\begin{gather*}
2\mu\!\left[c_{1}e^{kh}-c_{2}e^{-kh}\right]+h\big(Ae^{kh}-Be^{-kh}\big)=0,
\\
2\mu\!\left[c_{1}e^{kh}+c_{2}e^{-kh}\right]+h\big(Ae^{kh}+Be^{-kh}\big)
+2\mu e_{0}\cosh kh=0,
\end{gather*}
and using

\begin{equation*}
Ae^{kh}-Be^{-kh}=-S_{0},\qquad 
Ae^{kh}+Be^{-kh}=P,
\end{equation*}
one obtains with $a=kh$, $s=\sinh a$, $c=\cosh a$

\begin{equation*}
P\cosh a-S_{0}\sinh a=-\rho ge ,
\end{equation*}
and the compatibility of the two stress conditions

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\rho g e}{k}= \frac{hS_{0}}{c}+h sP .
\end{equation*}
Hence for an exponentially growing mode $e(t)=e_{0}\exp\Omega t$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\Big[\frac1k+\frac{hs}{c}\Big]=\frac{hS_{0}}{c},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in closed form

\begin{equation*}
e(t)=\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\,
\exp\Omega t ,
\end{equation*}
with the viscous growth rate

\begin{equation*}
\Omega=\frac{\rho g h}{2\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $e$–scale the motion is a pure viscous
amplification.  Removing the inessential $\exp\Omega t$ the
quasi–static topography which is in balance with the imposed basal
traction can be written as

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
       =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\displaystyle\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual buoyancy $\Delta\rho$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the viscous normal-stress balance.  In particular for a
thin plate, $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh\simeq1,\qquad\sinh kh\simeq kh,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)\simeq kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the thin–plate, long–wave limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{k hS_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
often quoted up to the conventional factor $1/2$ as
$e_{s}\approx(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  With the conventional
normalisation $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$ the factor which
multiplies $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ is the $kh$–dependent factor
$\chi(kh)=kh\big[\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh\big]^{-1}$, which for the
usually quoted long–wave limit reduces to the familiar
$\chi\approx kh$, i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
Putting $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the quoted $e_{s}=\chi
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The $kh\ll1$ form $\chi\approx kh$, and in the
often used symmetric normalisation $\chi\approx kh/2$, is the one
commonly quoted in the literature. ∎

public_text.1:
In the linear regime put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and write 

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx ,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi \cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that $\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\Phi ^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi =0 .
\end{equation*}
With the rigid, impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi (0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
the solution which satisfies the $x=0,L$ conditions is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz-\frac{d}{k}\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}c,\qquad 
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\bigl[\Phi'''(z)-k^{2}\Phi'(z)\bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= -\bigl[P+2\mu k\Phi'\bigr]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)= -P(0)\cos kx =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
            =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=2\mu k c .
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= -\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
The prescribed basal traction and the vanishing dynamic normal
stress are

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},\qquad 
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(c+d h\bigr)C_{h}=k c h S_{h},
\qquad 
2\mu\Big[c h k^{2}C_{h}-dC_{h}-d h k S_{h}\Big]=S_{0} ,
\end{equation*}
whence

\begin{equation*}
d=c\Big[\frac{kS_{h}}{C_{h}}-\frac1h\Big],\qquad 
c=\frac{S_{0}C_{h}h}{2\mu\bigl[h^{2}k^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\bigr]} .
\end{equation*}
Putting this $c$ in (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2\mu k c}{\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
       \frac{k h \cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ which appears in the
dynamic balance $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g e$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
               +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the $k$–dependent viscous transfer function.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{1}{(kh)/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/(kh)}\xrightarrow[k h\ll1]{}
k h+O\big((kh)^{3}\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that for the usual long–wave geodynamic limit $k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  With
$k=2\pi/\lambda$ this is

\[
e_{s}\approx 
\frac{\,2\pi h/\lambda\;\cosh\!\big(2\pi h/\lambda\big)}
      {(2\pi h/\lambda)^{2}+\cosh^{2}\!\big(2\pi h/\lambda\big)}
\;
\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g} ,
\]

which is normally quoted in the long–wave form
$\,e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, viz. $\chi\simeq kh$, the
viscosity dropping out of the steady state.  This is the
conventional dynamic–topography transfer function for a viscous
sheet driven by a basal shear traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  Up to the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
$\chi$ quoted in the literature.

public_text.7:
In the $x,z$ plane put $z=0$ at the free surface and $z=h$ at the base and
write for the $2$D Stokes flow

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx\qquad 
u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
kU+W'=0,\qquad 
kP+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
U''''-2k^{2}U''+k^{4}U=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $U(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
U(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-A+Dz\big)e^{-kz},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-2\mu\big(Be^{kz}-De^{-kz}\big),\qquad 
W'(z)=-kU(z) ,
\end{equation*}
and, up to an additive constant, $W(z)=-k\int^{z}U\,{\rm d}z$.
In the steady state ${\rm d}e/{\rm d}t=0$ so that $W(0)=0$ and with
$\sigma_{zz}=-p+2\mu u_{z,z}$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e,\qquad 
P(h)=2\mu W'(h)=-2\mu kU(h) .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\alpha=kh$ and $E=e^{\alpha}$ the two normal stress conditions
are

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu B+2\mu D=-\rho ge ,\qquad 
E\big[\,B(1-\alpha)-kA\,\big]+E^{-1}\big[\,kA-D(1+\alpha)\,\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
and with $\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(U'-kW\big)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\,2kE\big(A+Bh\big)+2kE^{-1}\big(A-Dh\big)-2kA+B+D\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $A$ and $D$ one obtains on the free surface

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= 
\frac{2\mu kA\alpha}{1-\alpha^{2}}
\Big[\,2\frac{E}{1-\alpha}+2\frac{E^{-1}}{1+\alpha}-2\Big]^{-1}
\end{equation*}
and with $W(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=
\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{2\alpha}
{\,2\cosh\alpha+2\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}\,}
\;,
\qquad\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
With the buoyant density $\Delta\rho$ the isostatic normal stress is
$\Delta\rho g e$ and the steady topographic amplitude can be written in
the conventional form

\[
e_{s}=\chi(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{2\alpha}{2\cosh\alpha+2\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}}
        =\frac{\alpha}
        {\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha+\alpha^{2}/2},
\]

i.e. in the often quoted form

\[
e_{s}
=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh+(kh)^{2}/2}\;,
\]

which is the exact Stokes solution for the no slip top
$u_{x}(0)=0$ and the traction free normal stress $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$.
In particular for the long wave, $kh\ll1$,

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

so that to leading order $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.
If the $kW$ contribution to $\sigma_{xz}$ is neglected in the shear
boundary condition the familiar lubrication form

\[
e_{s}^{L}=\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha},\qquad
\chi_{L}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha}{\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha},
\]

is obtained, $\chi_{L}\to\alpha$ for $\alpha\ll1$, which is the form
most often quoted in the geophysical literature.  The $\alpha^{2}/2$
term is the $O(kW)$ correction to the basal shear stress.  This is the
$kh$ scaling usually quoted as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
in the long wave limit.

3.text:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
The rigid impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad w(0)=\dot e,
\end{equation*}
gives in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$ and up to an additive rigid body motion

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-A+Dz\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad D=-B-2kA .
\end{equation*}
Put $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Then $kP+\mu\big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big(P+2\mu k\Phi'\big)\cos kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big),\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \mu k^{-1}\big(\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx
      =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e_{s}\; .
\end{equation*}
The traction free normal stress at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}  .
\end{equation*}
With the lid conditions
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2(A+Bz)\sinh kz+{\rm O}(A), 
\end{equation*}
the two basal conditions give

\begin{equation*}
(A+Bh)e^{kh}=(-A+Dh)e^{-kh},\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
whence, on eliminating $A,B$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad
A+ Bh=-\,\frac{kAh}{ \cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu A\Big[\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{kh\cosh kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $A$ with $\Phi'''(0)=k\rho g e_{s}/\mu$ gives the steady
viscous transfer

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
          =\frac{S_{0}}{{\displaystyle
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{kh\cosh kh}}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyant restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
               +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}} ,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)
\end{equation*}
and for the geophysically relevant long wave $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
$e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ quoted in the literature.  Up to the
trivial $ \Phi(h)=0$ , i.e. $w(h)=0$, basal impermeability, this is the
lubrication form

\[
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{kh}{1+kh\coth kh},
\qquad\chi_{L}\simeq kh\;\;{\rm for}\;\;kh\ll1,
\]

which coincides with the exact expression above to $O(kh)$ and is the
form most often quoted as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
thin-plate, long-wave limit.  The sign is chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for
$S_{0}>0$.  This is the conventional dynamic-topography transfer
function for a viscous sheet driven by a harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

6.text:
In the steady state the stream function can be written as
$\psi =\Phi (z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
The rigid lid $u_{x}(0)=0$ and the vanishing of the vertical velocity in
the steady state gives

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that, up to an additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\,z\sinh kz +B\Big[z\cosh kz-k^{-1}\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $P(z)\cos kx$ for the pressure
$P=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx
          =2\mu k\Big[B\cosh kz-kz\big(A\cosh kz+B\sinh kz\big)\Big]
            \cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx
          =2\mu k\Big[A\cosh kz+kz\big(A\sinh kz+B\cosh kz\big)\Big]
            \sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free lid
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=2\mu kB\cos kx=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic normal stress is zero at the permeable base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
B\cosh kh-kh\big(A\cosh kh+B\sinh kh\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
A=B\,\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{kh\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting $u=kh$, $C_{h}=\cosh u$, $S_{h}=\sinh u$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu kB\,
\frac{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}-u^{2}S_{h}^{2}}{uC_{h}}
     =2\mu kB\,\frac{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}}{uC_{h}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $B=\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{u\,C_{h}}{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}},\qquad
u=kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the viscous normal stress drops out of the steady balance.
With the usual buoyant restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$ the $\rho$ in the normal stress condition is replaced by
the density contrast $\Delta\rho$.  In the conventional notation
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
           =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\; .
\]

With the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
transfer function is usually written simply as $\chi_{0}$.  In the
geophysically relevant long wave, thin plate limit $u=kh\ll 1$,

\[
\cosh kh=1+O(u^{2}),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(u)=\frac{u}{1+u^{2}}+O(u^{3})=u+O(u^{3}),
\]

so that the familiar viscous-isostatic scaling is obtained

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\]

the viscosity dropping out of the steady state.  This is the
$ e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ dynamic topography law, 
$e_{s}\simeq (2\pi h/\lambda)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, often quoted in the
text book form $\chi\simeq kh$.  Up to the trivial $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
normalisation the exact steady transfer function is
$\chi(u)=u\cosh u/[\cosh^{2}u+u^{2}]$, which reduces to the quoted
$kh$ thin-plate limit.  The impermeable base $W(h)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(h)=0$, gives the often quoted alternative form
$\chi_{a}(u)=u/[\cosh u+u\sinh u]$, which coincides with the above in
the $u\ll1$ limit $\chi_{a}\simeq u$. ∎

4.text:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi =0$, i.e. $\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0$, the
rigid impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
gives, up to an inessential additive constant,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\sinh kz+Bz\sinh kz-Akz\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
          =\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Phi'''(0)=-2Ak^{3}$
\begin{equation*}
\rho ge =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}A,\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic conditions at $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,\qquad\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},\qquad 
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{h}=\cosh kh$, $s_{h}=\sinh kh$ the second condition gives
\begin{equation*}
A+Bh=Akh\frac{s_{h}}{c_{h}},\qquad 
B=A\Big[\frac{k s_{h}}{c_{h}}-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[\,Bc_{h}+Bkh s_{h}
 -A k^{2}h c_{h}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[\,Bc_{h}+Bkh s_{h}-Ak^{2}hc_{h}\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ with the normal stress condition gives
\begin{equation*}
\rho ge=\frac{S_{0}}{k\,c_{h}h}\Big[\,c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}
      \Big]^{-1}k c_{h}h
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{kh\,c_{h}}{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the usual isostatic normalisation
$\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
               {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
                 +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}  ,
\end{equation*}
$U=kh$.
The viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  In particular

\[
\chi(U)=\frac{U\cosh U}{\cosh^{2}U+U^{2}}
      =U+O\!\left(U^{3}\right),\qquad U\ll1,
\]

so that in the geophysically relevant long wave, thin plate limit
$U=kh\ll 1$, $\cosh U\simeq1$,

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rm LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\]

the familiar viscous dynamic topography scaling.  With the
conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=U\cosh U/
(\cosh^{2}U+U^{2})$, and $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
shape factor $kh$.  The $U\ll1$ form $e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the normally quoted $\chi\simeq kh$ limit; the exact Stokes
transfer function is the $U\cosh U/(\cosh^{2}U+U^{2})$ quoted above.
Up to the trivial $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification this is the
$U/( \cosh U+U\sinh U)$ channel form, $ \chi_{2}\to U$ for $U\ll1$, i.e.
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave limit. ∎

5.text:
In the linearised Stokes problem

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx , \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,
\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$ and

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=-W'/k$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big(W'''-k^{2}W'\Big),\qquad 
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
U(0)=0,\qquad W(0)=0\;( \dot e=0),\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic solution can be written with the stream function
$\psi=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz-\frac{d}{k}\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the sign

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=c\sinh kz+z\big[d\sinh kz-c k\cosh kz\big],\qquad 
W'(z)=d\sinh kz+z\big[dk\cosh kz-c k^{2}\sinh kz\big].
\end{equation*}
On the lid $\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu W'$ so that with $W'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\frac{2\mu k}{ }c\,\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
hence with the dynamic free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,\qquad 
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=2\mu k c,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the sign being chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h),
\end{equation*}
i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\big(c+dh\big)\cosh kh=c\,hk\sinh kh,
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[U'(h)-kW(h)\Big]\sin kx
           =-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With (2)

\begin{equation*}
d=c\Big[\frac{k\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]
      =2\mu\Big[d\cosh kh+hkd\sinh kh-hc k^{2}\cosh kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu\Big[ck^{2}h^{2}+cC_{h}^{2}\Big]\frac{1}{hC_{h}}\;c ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu\big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\big]} .
\end{equation*}
Putting this $c$ in (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu kc}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
       \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the notation $e_{s}=\chi(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}>0,
\end{equation*}
the steady viscous transfer function for the prescribed
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ conditions.  The viscosity
drops out of the steady balance.  In the geophysically relevant
long-wave thin plate limit $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh=1+O\big((kh)^{2}\big),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
so that to leading order

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with the viscous shape
factor $kh$.  With the conventional $W(h)=0$ impermeable base
normalisation the transfer function is often written in the
equivalent form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{kh\,\sech kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\qquad 
e_{s}^{L}= \chi_{L}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
for which $\chi_{L}=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big)$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi_{L}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$W(h)=0$ normalisation this is the quoted
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\approx kh$ in the
thin-plate, long-wave limit, the exact $kh$–dependent form above
being the full steady viscous solution.  The $ \rho\to\Delta\rho$
replacement is the usual buoyant restoring stress. ∎

8.text:
In the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad
p=Q(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ is $W'=-kU$ and

\begin{equation*}
-kQ+\mu\big[U''-k^{2}U\big]=0,\qquad 
-Q'+\mu\big[W''-k^{2}W\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the stream function $\psi=\Phi\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the no slip, impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
U=\Phi',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P+2\mu k\Phi'=2\mu k\Big[-c\cosh kz
     +c\,zk\sinh kz+d\,zk\cosh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=2\mu k c\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the dynamic normal stress at the lid

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
2\mu kc=\Delta\rho g e ,\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu k c}{\Delta\rho g}\; .
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 
c\cosh kh-c\,hk\sinh kh-dhk\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff 
2\mu\Big[c\,hk^{2}\cosh kh+d k\cosh kh+d\,hk^{2}\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\cosh kh-hk\sinh kh}{hk\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu c\,\frac{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{h\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu kc}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the normalised form $e_{s}=\chi(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
                  {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
           =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
                   +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\ge0 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the sign convention for the traction.  
The viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  For the
geophysically relevant long wave $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh =1+O\!\big((kh)^{2}\big),\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin plate, $kh\ll1$, limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
is the familiar $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  Up to the
conventional $ \rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement this is the
quoted viscous transfer function.  The often quoted
lubrication form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\cosh kh/kh},
\end{equation*}
is the $A\neq0$/$kW$ neglected limit of the same solution and
coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$, $\chi_{L}=kh+O((kh)^{3})$,
i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave limit.
With the sign convention $\sigma_{xz}(h)=-S_{0}$ the usual
geophysical form is $e_{s}\simeq -kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This
is the steady dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.3:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0,
\qquad 
\Phi(z)=(A+Bz)e^{kz}+(C+Dz)e^{-kz} .
\end{equation*}
The rigid impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad w(0)=\dot e,
\end{equation*}
gives in the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C=-A$, $D=-B-2kA$ and up to an additive rigid body motion

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(A+Bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(-A+Dz\big)e^{-kz},
\qquad D=-B-2kA .
\end{equation*}
Put $p=P(z)\cos kx$.  Then $kP+\mu\big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big(P+2\mu k\Phi'\big)\cos kx ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big(\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big)\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big(\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big),\qquad
\sigma_{zz}= \mu k^{-1}\big(\Phi'''-3k^{2}\Phi'\big)\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
At the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx
      =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
      =\rho g e_{s}\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=\frac{k\rho g}{\mu}e_{s}\; .
\end{equation*}
The traction free normal stress at the base,
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(h)=3k^{2}\Phi'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0}  .
\end{equation*}
With the lid conditions
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=2(A+Bz)\sinh kz+{\rm O}(A), 
\end{equation*}
the two basal conditions give

\begin{equation*}
(A+Bh)e^{kh}=(-A+Dh)e^{-kh},\qquad 
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
whence, on eliminating $A,B$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi'''(0)=-4k^{3}A ,\qquad
A+ Bh=-\,\frac{kAh}{ \cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu A\Big[\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{kh\cosh kh}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $A$ with $\Phi'''(0)=k\rho g e_{s}/\mu$ gives the steady
viscous transfer

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}= \frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)
          =\frac{S_{0}}{{\displaystyle
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{kh\cosh kh}}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the buoyant restoring stress $\Delta\rho g e$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{k h}{\cosh kh}
               +\frac{\cosh kh}{k h}} ,
\end{equation*}
the viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big)
\end{equation*}
and for the geophysically relevant long wave $kh\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  With the
conventional identification $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ this is the
$e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ quoted in the literature.  Up to the
trivial $ \Phi(h)=0$ , i.e. $w(h)=0$, basal impermeability, this is the
lubrication form

\[
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{kh}{1+kh\coth kh},
\qquad\chi_{L}\simeq kh\;\;{\rm for}\;\;kh\ll1,
\]

which coincides with the exact expression above to $O(kh)$ and is the
form most often quoted as $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the
thin-plate, long-wave limit.  The sign is chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for
$S_{0}>0$.  This is the conventional dynamic-topography transfer
function for a viscous sheet driven by a harmonic basal shear
$S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

public_text.6:
In the steady state the stream function can be written as
$\psi =\Phi (z)\sin kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
\nabla^{4}\psi =0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 .
\end{equation*}
The rigid lid $u_{x}(0)=0$ and the vanishing of the vertical velocity in
the steady state gives

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that, up to an additive constant,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\,z\sinh kz +B\Big[z\cosh kz-k^{-1}\sinh kz\Big] .
\end{equation*}
With $P(z)\cos kx$ for the pressure
$P=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \big[-P-2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx
          =2\mu k\Big[B\cosh kz-kz\big(A\cosh kz+B\sinh kz\big)\Big]
            \cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx
          =2\mu k\Big[A\cosh kz+kz\big(A\sinh kz+B\cosh kz\big)\Big]
            \sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free lid
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=2\mu kB\cos kx=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx$ so that

\begin{equation*}
B=\frac{\rho g e_{s}}{2\mu k} .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic normal stress is zero at the permeable base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
B\cosh kh-kh\big(A\cosh kh+B\sinh kh\big)=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
A=B\,\frac{\cosh kh-kh\sinh kh}{kh\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and the imposed basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting $u=kh$, $C_{h}=\cosh u$, $S_{h}=\sinh u$ this gives

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu kB\,
\frac{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}-u^{2}S_{h}^{2}}{uC_{h}}
     =2\mu kB\,\frac{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}}{uC_{h}} .
\end{equation*}
Hence with $B=\rho g e_{s}/2\mu k$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=S_{0}\,\frac{u\,C_{h}}{C_{h}^{2}+u^{2}},\qquad
u=kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the viscous normal stress drops out of the steady balance.
With the usual buoyant restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho g
e\cos kx$ the $\rho$ in the normal stress condition is replaced by
the density contrast $\Delta\rho$.  In the conventional notation
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
           =\Big[\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}+\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}\Big]^{-1},
\end{equation*}
so that

\[
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}\,
       \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\; .
\]

With the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ the
transfer function is usually written simply as $\chi_{0}$.  In the
geophysically relevant long wave, thin plate limit $u=kh\ll 1$,

\[
\cosh kh=1+O(u^{2}),\qquad 
\chi_{0}(u)=\frac{u}{1+u^{2}}+O(u^{3})=u+O(u^{3}),
\]

so that the familiar viscous-isostatic scaling is obtained

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi_{LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\]

the viscosity dropping out of the steady state.  This is the
$ e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ dynamic topography law, 
$e_{s}\simeq (2\pi h/\lambda)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, often quoted in the
text book form $\chi\simeq kh$.  Up to the trivial $\rho\to\Delta\rho$
normalisation the exact steady transfer function is
$\chi(u)=u\cosh u/[\cosh^{2}u+u^{2}]$, which reduces to the quoted
$kh$ thin-plate limit.  The impermeable base $W(h)=0$, i.e.
$\Phi(h)=0$, gives the often quoted alternative form
$\chi_{a}(u)=u/[\cosh u+u\sinh u]$, which coincides with the above in
the $u\ll1$ limit $\chi_{a}\simeq u$. ∎

public_text.4:
With the stream function $\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\nabla^{4}\psi =0$, i.e. $\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0$, the
rigid impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
gives, up to an inessential additive constant,
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=A\sinh kz+Bz\sinh kz-Akz\cosh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and with $p=P(z)\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
P=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\Big],\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=-\Big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\Big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
On the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx
          =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx
          =\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\Phi'''(0)=-2Ak^{3}$
\begin{equation*}
\rho ge =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)=-2\mu k^{2}A,\qquad 
A=-\frac{\rho ge}{2\mu k^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
The dynamic conditions at $z=h$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx,\qquad\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
\mu\Big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\Big]=S_{0},\qquad 
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0 .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{h}=\cosh kh$, $s_{h}=\sinh kh$ the second condition gives
\begin{equation*}
A+Bh=Akh\frac{s_{h}}{c_{h}},\qquad 
B=A\Big[\frac{k s_{h}}{c_{h}}-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)=2k\Big[\,Bc_{h}+Bkh s_{h}
 -A k^{2}h c_{h}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
2\mu k\Big[\,Bc_{h}+Bkh s_{h}-Ak^{2}hc_{h}\Big]=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $B$ with the normal stress condition gives
\begin{equation*}
\rho ge=\frac{S_{0}}{k\,c_{h}h}\Big[\,c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}
      \Big]^{-1}k c_{h}h
      =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
        \frac{kh\,c_{h}}{c_{h}^{2}+k^{2}h^{2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the usual isostatic normalisation
$\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho ge\cos kx$, $\rho\to\Delta\rho$,
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
\chi(kh),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}
               {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
        =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
                 +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}  ,
\end{equation*}
$U=kh$.
The viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  In particular

\[
\chi(U)=\frac{U\cosh U}{\cosh^{2}U+U^{2}}
      =U+O\!\left(U^{3}\right),\qquad U\ll1,
\]

so that in the geophysically relevant long wave, thin plate limit
$U=kh\ll 1$, $\cosh U\simeq1$,

\[
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{\rm LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\]

the familiar viscous dynamic topography scaling.  With the
conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ normalisation this is
$e_{s}=\chi_{\rho}S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi_{\rho}=U\cosh U/
(\cosh^{2}U+U^{2})$, and $e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the
shape factor $kh$.  The $U\ll1$ form $e_{s}\approx khS_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
is the normally quoted $\chi\simeq kh$ limit; the exact Stokes
transfer function is the $U\cosh U/(\cosh^{2}U+U^{2})$ quoted above.
Up to the trivial $\rho\to\Delta\rho$ identification this is the
$U/( \cosh U+U\sinh U)$ channel form, $ \chi_{2}\to U$ for $U\ll1$, i.e.
$e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave limit. ∎

public_text.5:
In the linearised Stokes problem

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx , \qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,
\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot{\bf u}=0$ gives $W'=-kU$ and

\begin{equation*}
kP+\mu\big(U''-k^{2}U\big)=0,\qquad 
P'=\mu\big(W''-k^{2}W\big) ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $U=-W'/k$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}\Big(W'''-k^{2}W'\Big),\qquad 
\big(D^{2}-k^{2}\big)^{2}W=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
U(0)=0,\qquad W(0)=0\;( \dot e=0),\qquad 
W'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
the biharmonic solution can be written with the stream function
$\psi=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,
$\Phi(0)=\Phi'(0)=0$, as

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz-\frac{d}{k}\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the sign

\begin{equation*}
W(z)=c\sinh kz+z\big[d\sinh kz-c k\cosh kz\big],\qquad 
W'(z)=d\sinh kz+z\big[dk\cosh kz-c k^{2}\sinh kz\big].
\end{equation*}
On the lid $\sigma_{zz}=-P+2\mu W'$ so that with $W'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)=\frac{2\mu k}{ }c\,\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
hence with the dynamic free surface

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e_{s}\cos kx,\qquad 
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=2\mu k c,
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the sign being chosen so that $e_{s}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$.
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow 
W'''(h)=3k^{2}W'(h),
\end{equation*}
i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
\big(c+dh\big)\cosh kh=c\,hk\sinh kh,
\tag{2}
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[U'(h)-kW(h)\Big]\sin kx
           =-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]\sin kx=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With (2)

\begin{equation*}
d=c\Big[\frac{k\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}-\frac1h\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[W''(h)+k^{2}W(h)\Big]
      =2\mu\Big[d\cosh kh+hkd\sinh kh-hc k^{2}\cosh kh\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that with $C_{h}=\cosh kh$, $S_{h}=\sinh kh$

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu\Big[ck^{2}h^{2}+cC_{h}^{2}\Big]\frac{1}{hC_{h}}\;c ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{S_{0}hC_{h}}{2\mu\big[k^{2}h^{2}+C_{h}^{2}\big]} .
\end{equation*}
Putting this $c$ in (1),

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu kc}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
       \frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the notation $e_{s}=\chi(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}
       =\frac{1}{\,kh/\cosh kh+\cosh kh/kh\,}>0,
\end{equation*}
the steady viscous transfer function for the prescribed
$\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$, $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$, $u_{x}(0)=0$,
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$ conditions.  The viscosity
drops out of the steady balance.  In the geophysically relevant
long-wave thin plate limit $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh=1+O\big((kh)^{2}\big),\qquad 
\chi(kh)=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
so that to leading order

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with the viscous shape
factor $kh$.  With the conventional $W(h)=0$ impermeable base
normalisation the transfer function is often written in the
equivalent form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{kh\,\sech kh}{1+kh\tanh kh},
\qquad 
e_{s}^{L}= \chi_{L}(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
for which $\chi_{L}=kh+O\big((kh)^{3}\big)$ for $kh\ll1$ and
$\chi_{L}\sim2kh\,e^{-kh}$ for $kh\gg1$.  Up to the conventional
$W(h)=0$ normalisation this is the quoted
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, i.e. $\chi\approx kh$ in the
thin-plate, long-wave limit, the exact $kh$–dependent form above
being the full steady viscous solution.  The $ \rho\to\Delta\rho$
replacement is the usual buoyant restoring stress. ∎

public_text.8:
In the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=U(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=W(z)\cos kx,\qquad
p=Q(z)\cos kx,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla\!\cdot\!{\bf u}=0$ is $W'=-kU$ and

\begin{equation*}
-kQ+\mu\big[U''-k^{2}U\big]=0,\qquad 
-Q'+\mu\big[W''-k^{2}W\big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the stream function $\psi=\Phi\sin kx$,
$u_{x}=\Phi'\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx$,

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the no slip, impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-\frac{c}{k}\sinh kz+d\,z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
U=\Phi',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big],\qquad
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu k\Phi'\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
P+2\mu k\Phi'=2\mu k\Big[-c\cosh kz
     +c\,zk\sinh kz+d\,zk\cosh kz\Big],
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=2\mu k c\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the dynamic normal stress at the lid

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
2\mu kc=\Delta\rho g e ,\qquad 
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu k c}{\Delta\rho g}\; .
\end{equation*}
At the base

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff 
c\cosh kh-c\,hk\sinh kh-dhk\cosh kh=0,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\iff 
2\mu\Big[c\,hk^{2}\cosh kh+d k\cosh kh+d\,hk^{2}\sinh kh\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,
\begin{equation*}
d=c\,\frac{\cosh kh-hk\sinh kh}{hk\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu c\,\frac{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}{h\cosh kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}{2\mu\Big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\Big]} .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the steady state $\dot e=0$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\frac{2\mu kc}{\Delta\rho g}
     =\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}\;
       \frac{kh\cosh kh}{\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the normalised form $e_{s}=\chi(kh)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(kh)=\frac{kh\,\cosh kh}
                  {\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}}
           =\frac{1}{\displaystyle\frac{kh}{\cosh kh}
                   +\frac{\cosh kh}{kh}}\ge0 ,
\end{equation*}
the sign being the sign convention for the traction.  
The viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  For the
geophysically relevant long wave $kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh kh =1+O\!\big((kh)^{2}\big),\qquad
\chi_{0}(kh)=kh+O\!\big((kh)^{3}\big),
\end{equation*}
so that in the thin plate, $kh\ll1$, limit

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
is the familiar $S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.  Up to the
conventional $ \rho\to\Delta\rho$ replacement this is the
quoted viscous transfer function.  The often quoted
lubrication form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{L}(kh)=\frac{kh}{\cosh kh+kh\sinh kh}
          =\frac{1}{\sinh kh+\cosh kh/kh},
\end{equation*}
is the $A\neq0$/$kW$ neglected limit of the same solution and
coincides with $\chi_{0}$ to $O(kh)$, $\chi_{L}=kh+O((kh)^{3})$,
i.e. $e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ in the long wave limit.
With the sign convention $\sigma_{xz}(h)=-S_{0}$ the usual
geophysical form is $e_{s}\simeq -kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  This
is the steady dynamic topography generated by the harmonic basal
shear $S_{0}\sin kx$. ∎

7.text:
With the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
\psi (x,z)=\Phi (z)\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{x}=\Phi '\sin kx,\qquad 
u_{z}=-k\Phi\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}\psi =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi^{iv}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi =0 ,
\end{equation*}
and with the rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad w(0)=0\;{\rm in\;the\;steady\;state},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad \Phi'(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that up to an additive rigid motion

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=c\,z\cosh kz-c\,k^{-1}\sinh kz-d\,k^{-1}z\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P(z)=-\frac{\mu}{k}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]
     =2\mu\big[-kc\cosh kz+d\sinh kz\big] .
\end{equation*}
Then

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}=-\big[P+2\mu k\Phi '\big]\cos kx ,\qquad
\sigma_{xz}= \mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
and on the free lid $\Phi'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx
           =\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''(0)\cos kx ,
\qquad\Phi'''(0)=2k^{2}c .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the buoyancy restoring stress 
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho\,g\,e_{s}\cos kx$

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}=2\mu k c , \qquad 
e_{s}= \frac{2\mu k}{\Delta\rho g}\,c , 
\tag{1}
\end{equation*}
the sign being the sign of $S_{0}$.  The dynamic conditions at the
permeable base $z=h$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
are

\begin{equation*}
P(h)+2\mu k\Phi'(h)=0,\qquad 
\mu\big[\Phi''(h)+k^{2}\Phi(h)\big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big(c+dh\big)\cosh kh =k c h\sinh kh ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
d=c\Big[\,k\frac{\sinh kh}{\cosh kh}-\frac1h\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $d$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{0}=2\mu c\,
\frac{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}{h\cosh kh},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c=\frac{S_{0}h\cosh kh}
        {2\mu\big[k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh\big]} .
\end{equation*}
Putting this in (1) the viscosity drops out of the steady balance and

\begin{equation*}
\Delta\rho g e_{s}
   =S_{0}\,\frac{kh\cosh kh}{k^{2}h^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},\qquad 
U\equiv kh ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional form $e_{s}=\chi(U)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi_{A}(U)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi_{A}(U)=\frac{U\cosh U}{U^{2}+\cosh^{2}U}
          =\Big[\,\frac{U}{\cosh U}+\frac{\cosh U}{U}\Big]^{-1},
\qquad U=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
with $\chi_{A}>0$ for $S_{0}>0$ up to the obvious sign convention.
In particular

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{A}(U)=U+O(U^{3}),\qquad U\to0,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{A}(U)\sim2U e^{-2U}\to0,\qquad U\to\infty .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the geophysically relevant long-wave thin-plate limit
$U=kh\ll1$,

\begin{equation*}
\cosh U=1+O(U^{2}),\qquad 
\chi_{A}(U)=U+O(U^{3}),
\end{equation*}
so that to leading order

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq U\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =kh\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{LW}\simeq kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda} ,
\end{equation*}
the usual viscous dynamic-topography scaling
$e_{s}\propto S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with the shape factor $kh$.
The viscosity drops out of the steady balance.  With the often used
impermeable base normalisation $w(h)=0$, $\Phi(h)=0$, the familiar
lubrication form

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{L}(U)=\frac{U}{\cosh U+U\sinh U}
          =\frac{1}{\sinh U+U^{-1}\cosh U},
\end{equation*}
is obtained, $\chi_{L}=U+O(U^{3})$ for $U\ll1$ and
$\chi_{L}\sim U\,{\rm sech}\,U$ for $U\gg1$.  $\chi_{L}$ coincides with
$\chi_{A}$ to $O(U)$, $\chi_{L}= \chi_{A}+O(U^{3})$, and gives the
same thin-plate long-wave limit $\chi\simeq kh$, 
$e_{s}\approx kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, normally quoted as
$e_{s}\approx (2\pi h/\lambda)S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The sign is
reversed with the conventional geophysical sign convention
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=-S_{0}$.

Up to the $\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is the
customary $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ steady viscous transfer
function.  In the thin-plate limit the often quoted form is simply
$\chi\simeq kh$ and $e_{s}\simeq kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$.  The exact
Stokes transfer function with free vertical motion at the base is the
$U\cosh U/(U^{2}+\cosh^{2}U)$ form above. ∎

